µm
1,2 $ Pileus not hydrophanous, either in young or old fruit bodies (when, however, only adult and old pilei hygrophanous, young buttons being non-hygrophanous, then ± small species with pileus pruinose, at least over dies, cf. C. candicans, p. 74, and C. pruinosa, p. 77)
1,17 $ Pileus already distinctly hygrophanous when young, fading regular and beginning from the centre
2,3 $ Pileus often ± greenish, otherwise brown to grey, or white, or buff, (always pruinose), odour strong, sweet, coumarine-like when fresh, or like that of Swedish turnips, and then persistent and honey-like in exsiccates, most spores in telrad, all of them obtuse- based, most elliptical, pinkish buff (9 B 2) or deep yellow (9 G 1—9 G 2) in deposit
2,4 $ Pileus of varying colour but never greenish- tinged, odour very variable, sometimes even sweet but usually dissimilar to that described above, all or practically spores single, with obtuse, acute, or confluent bases, of different shapes, white or very pale yellow (as deep as 9 B 1, at most) in deposit
3,0 $ Pileus medium to very large, brown to grey, rarely ± white, odour turnip-Like, exsiccates fragrant, too, spores 3.2—1,3  µm broad, deep yellow in deposit 4 $ C. nebularis (p. 57)
3,0 $ Pileus medium-sized, mostly aerugineous, sometimes buff, or ± white, odour strongly coumaríne-like when fresh but exsiccates ± odourless, spores ca. 3.6-—.5.0 µm broad, pinkish buff in deposit 23.$ C. odora (P- 84)
4,5 $ Lamellae rich-coloured, pale cinnamon brown in adult fruit bodies, basal tomentum dearly brownish, spores small, up to 6.5 µm long and 4.0 µm broad, apiculus up to ca. 0.6 µm broad (clamp connections present at each septum)
4,6 $ Lamellae white to pale brownish or greyish, basal tomentum white to whitish, spores on the average distinctly longer and/or broader than above (when as small, clamps none to few)
5,0 $ Odour and taste ± fungoid, spores 4.2—6.0 x 2.9—3.3 µm all obtuse-based, elliptical to oblong, or ovate 14. $ C. gilvaoides (p. 71}
5,0 $ Odour and taste farinaceous, more slender species than above and with thinner flesh, spores 5.0—6.5 x 3.0—3.7 µm rather variable in shape, obtuse- or acute-based, many ob- ovate or lacrymoid. 15  $ C. gracilis (p. 72)
6,0 $ Pileus and Stipe bright-coloured being red- brown to almost orange, lamellae ± white, odour and taste strongly farinaceous, ± conspicuous rhizoids and mycelial hairs present, spores very large, 7.5—11.5 x 4.8—6.1 µm, all of them obtuse-based, most elliptical, none laerymoid13. $ C. sinopica (p. 69)
6,7 $ Colours usually less bright, odour and taste never farinaceous, conspicuous rhizoids and mycelial hairs mostly lacking (when present, spores less than 4.2 µm broad), spore morphological characters very variable, shape often lacrymoid
7,8 $ Pileus not (at least distinctly) pruinose, lamellae usually ± paler than pileus, odour very often agreeable, a few to all spores with acute or confluent bases, often lacrymoid (sometimes elliptical or subfusiform), apiculus/ apicular region very broad, 0.7—1.3 µm in diameter, occurring in summer especially, sometimes in alpine and subalpine vegetation
7,15 $ Pileus pruinose (always ?), lamellae in fresh fruit body ± concolorous with pileus, odour not agreeable, all spores obtuse-based, most always broadest in the middle, never lacrymoid, apiculus small to medium-sized (ca. 0.3 —0,7 µm broad), in autumn from the middle of August on, never in alpine or subalpine conditions
8,0 $ Pileus dark brown, flesh hygrophanous, only a minor part of spores acute-based, confluent- based ones lacking, elliptical, subfusiform etc. and broadest in the middle (sometimes a very few ± lacrymoid) 5. $ C. ciainpes (p. 59)
8,9 $ ' Colours never dark brown, flesh not hydrophanous, most spores confluent- and/or acute- based (confluent-based spores always present) and lacrymoid being broadest near apex 9
9,10 $ Pileus surface chestnut brown with KOH, flesh rather thin
9,11 $ KOH reaction of pileus surface negative (see p. 30, too), flesh thick to thin
10,0 $ Pileus surface scaly, at least over disc, and mat, stipe usually rather long (4—9 cm), lamellae whitish, contrasting in colour with the distinctly darker stipe, spores larger, 6.0— 9.0(—11.0) x 3.2—5.0(—6,5) µm 10. $ C. squamulosa (p. 65)
10,0 $ Pileus surface smooth (rarely slightly areolate over disc) and usually ± shining in exsiccates, stipe rather short, lamellae becoming pale brownish, their colour not contrasting markedly with that of stipe, spores rather small, 5.5—8.0 x 3.1—4-.0 µm $ C. bresadolïana (p. 67)
11,0 $ Stipe very tall, 10—16 x 15—30, distinctly (ca. 1.5 to 4 times) longer than diameter of pileus, odour strong, reminiscent of oil of bitter almonds, most spores subglobose in contour, lactifers abundant in gill trama 6. $ C. geotropa (p. 60
11,12 $ Stipe distinctly shorter and thinner, also shorter in relation to pileus diameter, odour fungoid or otherwise unlike that described above (at least fainter), spores usually more elongated, lactiferous hyphae few or absent in gill trama
12,0 $ Pileus usually not infundibuliform, surface distinctly to indistinctly squamulose, usually except over disc, some times only areolate or radiantly fibrillose, flesh thick, spores 6.0— 11.0 x 4.2•—6.2 µm, part of them acute- based and almost obovate or even elliptical (subalpine to alpine) 12, $ C. lapponica (P- 68)
12,13 $ Pileus mostly infundibuliform, sometimes almost plane, surface completely smooth, flesh rather thin to very thin, spores often smaller than above, practically ail of them with confluent bases, ± lacrymoid, none elliptical
13,0 $ Pileus whitish, spores large, broad, 6.8—9.6 (—10.8) x (4.2—)4.6—6,8 µm, tapering very abruptly towards the apicular region 8, $ C. catinui (p. 63)
13,14 $ Pileus colour different shades of brown (only very rarely whitish), spores shorter and narrower, (4.2—}5.5—8.0(—10.0) x (2.4—) 3.4—5.4(-—6.4) µm, usually tapering ± gradually towards the apicular region
14,0 $ Pileus ± infundibuliform (rarely plane), surface not wrinkled nor shining nor yellow in exsiccates but mat and usually tan to some- what darker brown (very rarely whitish), lamellae thin and ± crowded, flesh not very thin, basidia medium-sized, not occurring above forest limit 9.$ C. gibba (p. 64)
14,0 $ Pileus ± plane, in exsiccates with rather striking yellow tones and ± shining wrinkled surface, lamellae thick and rather distant, flesh very thin, basidia very large (25—40 x 5.0—7.5 µm, in alpine Dryas vegetation 7, $ C. altaica (p. 62)
15,0 $ Lamellae very narrow and crowded being ca. 3—5 mm at broadest point, spores only 4.5— 6.5 x 2,9—3,6 µm, clamp connections totally absent or very scattered 2, $ C. karperi (p. 54)
15,16 $ Lamellae broad, being ca. 4—12 mm at broadest point, not crowded, spores clearly longer and/or broader than above, clamp connections at each septum
16,0 $ Pileus areolate, stipe diameter ca, 12—50 mm, rhizoids and mycelial hairs absent or not conspicuous, spores 5.5-—-7.5{—8.7) x 3.5—5.0 µm, most of them broadly elliptical I. $ C. alexandri (p. 53)
16,0 $ Pileus not areolate, stipe diameter ca. 7—13 mm, rather conspicuous rhizoids and mycelial hairs usually present, spores 7.5—10.5 x 3.0 —4.2 µm, ± fusiform (part of them rarely narrowly elliptical to oblong) 3. $ C. mornata (p. 55)
17,18 $ Conspicuous strigose ± hyaline mycelial hairs present at stipe base, often white rhizoids, too
17,23 $ Strigose mycelial hairs absent, only tomentum present (rarely a few inconspicuous, thin rhizoids, too)
18,19 $ Pileus pruinose or not, not translucent-stríate at margin, often not conspicuously wrinkled when dry, conspicuous white rhizoids present, lamellae ± buff, spores never constricted, in late autumn, winter, and especially throughout the spring, even in June
18,20 $ Pileus never pruinose, translucent-striate at margin, surface very conspicuously concentrically wrinkled in exsiccates, white rhizoids lacking (or very few and thin), lamellae pale brownish to greyish, spores often with asymmetrical and imperfect median constriction, in autumn only
19,0 $ Pileus not pruinose, flesh-coloured when moist (± alutaceous when faded and when dry), stipe stouter, ca. 5—15 mm in diameter, spores shorter and narrower, 3,8—5.5(—7.0) x 1.9—3.0(—3.5) µm, most of them narrowly elliptical to oblong, apiculus inconspicuous, occurring in May and June 21, $ C. rhizophora (p. 80)
19,0 $ Pileus pruinose (indistinctly when old; check overdisc), its colour somewhat dependent on age but never flesh-coloured, stipe si en de r, ca, 2—5 mm in diameter, spores 4.5—6.5 (—7.5) x 2.5—3.8 µm, most of them obovate to elliptical, apiculus long and often deform- ed, from late autumn to end of May 20. $ C. pruinosa (p. 77)
20,0 $ Pileus 2.5—-6,5 cm broad, chestnut brown, umbilicate, stipe tapering upwards, spores ca, 5.8—8.4 x 3.4—4.8  µm, most of them ± obovate to lacrymoid (rarely elliptical) 40. $ C. strigosa (p. 109)
20,21 $ Pileus somewhat smaller and mostly paler than above, usually not umbilicate but only depressed, stipe equal, spores shorter and/or thinner, most ovate or elliptical to oblong, a very few obovate, none lacrymoid
21,0 $ Taste bitter, spores 6.2—8.0 x 3.0—4,0  µm, most narrowly elliptic-oblong, the rest narrowly ovate 42, $ C. schulmannii (P. 112) 47
21,22 $ Taste mild, spores smaller, ranging from 4.2 to 7.0( -8.0) µm in length and from 2.8 to 3.6 µmin breadth, most of them ovate (especially in face view)
22,0 $ Pileus ± dark brown, indistinctly stríate, odour always fungoid or somewhat musty, never farinaceous, spores 5.5—7,0 {—8.0) x 2,8—3,3 µm  M $ C. fennica (p. 111)
22,0 $ Pileus pale brown, distinctly striate, odour always faintly farinaceous in young basidiocarps, spores shorter, 4.2—6.0 x 3,0—3.6  µm 43. $ C. lohjaensis (P. H2)
23,24 $ Odour (at least when bruised) and taste farinaceous (colours dull, also in lamellae)
23,29 $ Odour (and taste) fungold, fragrant etc., but never farinaceous
24,25 $ Spores only 3.0—4.2(—5.5) x 2.3—3.1 µm, pileus very often pruinose and non-striate, rarely non-pruinose and striate
24,26 $ Spores distinctly larger, pileus mostly non- pruinose and translucent-striate, only young buttons sometimes pruinose and non-striate
25,0 $ Colours usually rich tones of grey, pileus pruinose, with thick, unrolled, ± pruinose and non-striate margin 38, $ C. ditopa v. ditopa (p. 105)
25,0 $ Colours with less grey and more brown tinges, pileus not pruinose (or very indistinctly so over disc), with thin non-pruinose and distinctly translucent-striate margin 38.a $ C. ditopa v. odorula (p. 106)
26,0 $ Lamellae not truly decurrent, but narrowly adnate (see Fig. 34), odour when drying faint, very agreeable resembling that of menthol, exsiccates having faint smell of fish 39. $ C. menthiodora (p. 107)
26,27 $ Lamellae decurrent to broadly adnate, fresh fruit bodies have farinaceous odour only, exsiccates odourless
27,0 $  Colours with brown tinges, spores 5.0—6.5 (—7.0) x 2.9—3.6 µm, a considerable part of them obovate to lacrymoid (the rest mainly elliptical) 37.C. langei (p. 104)
27,28 $ Colours with predominantly grey tinges, length of spores exceeding that in 27 to greater or lesser degree and breadth sometimes also greater, most of them ovate or elliptic-oblong (a very few obovate, none lacrymoid)
28,0 $ Spores 5.0—7,0(—7,5) x 2.9—3.6 µm, all obtuse-based, most (usually) narrowly elliptical to- oblong, the rest (sometimes most) being mainly ovate, apiculus small, ca. 0.6— 0.8 x 0.4—0.6 µm 36 $ C. vïbecina (p, 103)
28,0 $ Spores 6.0—8.0(—9.0) x 3,0—4.2(—5.0) µm, rather variable in size and shape, a minor part of them acute-based, most of them ovate, a few fusiform, apiculus large, ca. 0.8—1.0 x 0,6—0.8 µm 35.$ C. orientalis (p. 103)
29,0 $ Spores 5.0—7.0(—8.0) x 3.0—4.2 µm, per- haps most of them confluent-based, without separate apiculus, and lacrymoid (the rest ± elliptical), apicular region ca. 0,5—0.9 [un in diameter, epicutis with conspicuous vesicles up to ca. 20 µm in diameter (colours of basidiocarp pale) 22.$ C. hydrogramma (p. 82)
29,30 $ Spores not confluent-based, shape variable, but only very rarely lacrymoid, apiculus always distinct, thinner than above, epicutis composed of filamentous hyphae only
30,31 $ Spores small, either 4.2—6.2 x 3.0—4.0 µm, and most ± subglobose to ± lacrymoid, mixed with a few spores of other shapes, or globose and 3.2—5.0 µm in diameter, pileus brown, not pruinose, very conspicuously concentrically wrinkled in exsiccates, lamellae (always ?) strikingly waxy and elastic in consistency, having more or less brownish tinge, odour fungoid
30,32 $ Spores elliptical, ovate, or oblong, when small usually thinner than above, pileus often pruinose, its surface in exsiccates usually not as above, odour often fragrant
31,0 $ Spores 4.2—6.2 x 3.0—4.0 µm, ± subglobose to ± lacrymoid etc., their bases being obtuse or acute, lamellae only pale brown in exsiccates, occurring from September to November in very meagre dry coniferous heath forests 33. $ C. subcordispora (p. 101
31,0 $ Spores irregularly globose, 3.2—5.0  µm in diameter, their bases obtuse, lamellae ferrugineous in exsiccates, in August 34-. $ C. globispora (p. 101)
32,33 $ Colours ± dull, grey to fuscous, sometimes pale brown, pileus not pruinose, lamellae not conspicuously cha n gin g colour when drying, odour either fungoid or like that of Cystoderma carcharías, or strongly fragrant, spores ± large, their range within ca. 5.5—9.5 x 3.2—4.8 µm
32,36 $ Colours pale, being white, buff, pale brown, or flesh-colour, pileus often pruinose, lamellae often changing colour when drying (often yellow in exsiccates), odour fungoid, or faintly fragrant, or ± acidulous, spores small, ca. 4,0—6.6 x 2.4—3.8 µm
33,0 $ Grey tints lacking or indistinct, odour strong and sweet, coumarine-like, spore deposit pinkish buff 29.$ C. fragrans (p. 93)
33,34 $ Grey tints present, mostly even predominant, in colours of fruit body, odour fungoid or like that of Cystoderma carcharías, spore deposit white or very pale yellow
34,0 $ Colours (even of lamellae) strikingly dark, pileus ± small, 1.5—4.5 cm broad, stipe rather slender, 2—6 mm in diameter, spores rather small, 5.5—7.5 x 3.2—4,2 µm(taste mild) 32.$ C. metachroides (P- 99)
34,35 $ Colours usually not so dark, especially lamellae being paler than above, pileus medium-sized, 3—7 cm broad, stipe not so slender, 3—9 mm in diameter, spores larger than above, ca. 6.4—9.5 x 3.5—4.8 µm
35,0 $ Stipe equal, ± one-coloured, lamellae rather narrowly decurrent, taste of adult and old pilei bitterish, spores 6.4—8.4(—9.4) x (3.1—)3.6—4,8 µm, very pale yellow in deposit 31. $ C. amarescens (p. 98)
35,0 $ Stipe usually enlarged downwards, its apex conspicuously paler than the rest of it, be- coming darker and greyer downwards, lamellae obliquely adnate to short decurrent, taste of ail parts always very mild, spores 6.4—9.5{—11,0) x 3.5—4.2(—4.6) µm, in deposit ± pure white 30. $ C. metachroa (p. 96)
36,37 $ Almost ail to distinct majority of spores occurring in tetrads (and dyads) in preparations made from lamellae, spore deposit pinkish buff
36,42 $ Spores single (rarely a minority in tetrads) in ail preparations, colour of spore deposit, when known, pure white
37,38 $ Pileus pruinose, not translucent-striate, flesh rather thick in disc
37,40 $ Pileus not pruinose (though extreme margin, often ± weakly so), translucent-striate, at least in old fruit bodies at margin, flesh less thick
38,0 $ Pileus very small to small, up to 4 cm broad, lamellae strikingly distant, in half-open habitats on boulders in moss 25. $ C. anísala (P. 89)
38,39 $ Pileus rather large to rather small, 3—10 cm broad, lamellae «normally« close, in forest litter
39,0 $ Pileus 3—7 cm broad, strongly and very regularly hygrophanous, ground colour some- what darker than above, disc often darker than the rest of pileus, stipe very elastic. long and slender, 3—6 mm in diameter, darker than above, flesh thinner than above 24. a $ C. phyllophila v. tenuis (p. 88)
39,0 $ Pileus 4—10 cm broad, rather weakly hydrophanous, ground colour when moist watery pale brownish, disc hardly darker, stipe not very elastic. ± stout, 4—15 mm in diameter, whitish to buff, flesh thick 24. $ C. phyllophila v. phyllophila (p. 86)
40,0 $ Pileus very pale, more or less beige through- out, margin not pruinose, al) parts turning faintly brownish when drying, in man-made habitats (road-sides, stubble-fields, pastures, etc.) on ± bare soil, sometimes together with low mosses 27. $ C. agrestis (p. 91)
40,41 $ Pileus darker than above and flesh-coloured, or, when equally pale, possessing distinctly darker disc, extreme margin very often pruinose, fruit body not turning brownish when drying, in ± meagre heath. forests, mostly in conifer litter
41,0 $ Pileus flesh-coloured throughout, margin ± thick, translucent-striate in old fruit bodies only, only very narrow pruinose border on extreme margin (usually evident in exsiccates, too), stipe usually equal, compressed in age, lamellae broadest around the middle, flesh very elastic. odour mostly lungoid (rarely faintly fragrant) 26. $ C. diatreta (p. 90)
41,0 $ Pileus paler, less reddish-tinted, ± clearly darker in disc, more distinctly striate than above {even when ± young), margin thinner, usually with 1—2 mm broad whitish pruinose zone, stipe usually tapering upwards and terete, lamellae usually broadest proximally (near stipe), flesh rather fragile, odour almost always faintly fragrant 28. $ C. marginella (P. 92)
42,43 $ In man-made habitats (road-sides, pastures, rubbish dumps, stubble-fields, lawns etc.) only, on ± bare soil, often together with low mosses, lamellae sordid pinkish or dirty whitish in exsiccates
42,44 $ In ± natural plant communities, either in woods or, rarely, above forest limit in alpine conditions, mostly in litter of deciduous and coniferous trees, rarely on bare soil (then near forest limit), lamellae yellow in exsiccates (occasionally whitish)
43,0 $ Lamellae narrower, and longer decurrent than above, dirty whitish in exsiccates, spores broader, 4.0—6.0 x 2.9—3.7 µm 18. $ C. ruderalis (p. 76)
43,0 $ Lamellae short decurrent, conspicuously sordid pinkish in exsiccates, spores 4.0—6.0 x 2.5—3.3, µm 17. $ C. dealbata (p. 751
44,0 $ Pileus whitish to pearl grey in exsiccates, spores 4.6—6.6(—7.8) x 2.6—3.6 µm, below forest limit, in litter 16. $ C. candicans (p. 74)
44,0 $ Pileus with ± distinct red tinges, e.g. sordid pinkish, in exsiccates, spores shorter and relatively broader, 4,0— 5.6 x 2.9—3.5 µm, in vicinity of forest limit, in both subalpine and alpine conditions, often on calcareous ± bare soil 19. $ C. laricicola (p. 76)
